From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tao Gong <gongtao0607@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7996: release HW_RRO sessions on teardown

The MT7996 HW_RRO (Hardware Receive Reordering Offload) engine allocates
a firmware-side reorder session per RX BlockAck agreement.  These sessions
are never freed on BA teardown, so over a long uptime the firmware leaks
HW_RRO sessions until the reorder engine enters a "weird state" and stalls
hardware RX offload (observed after ~a day even with the sub-64-window
bypass of patch 032 applied).

Free the session at IEEE80211_AMPDU_RX_STOP via
mt7996_mcu_wed_rro_reset_sessions().

The firmware-assigned session id is only known asynchronously: it arrives
in the UNI_WED_RRO_BA_SESSION_STATUS event, which runs in a different
context from the BA teardown path.  Reading the id straight from
rcu_dereference(wcid->aggr[tid])->id at teardown races that event handler
and dereferences an mt76_rx_tid that mt76_rx_aggr_stop() is about to free.
Instead, cache the id into a driver-owned mt7996_sta_link.rro_id[tid] when
the event arrives, and snapshot+clear it under dev->mt76.mutex before
mt76_rx_aggr_stop() runs, so teardown always has a stable id and never
touches freed reorder state.

The status event is delivered as a batch of per-session records.  The old
handler bailed out of the whole batch (break) on the first record whose
BA session is not tracked on the primary link (an MLO secondary link, or a
session already torn down between firmware queuing the event and the driver
processing it), silently dropping every remaining session id in the batch
and leaking those sessions.  Keep draining the batch (skb_pull + continue)
so every session id is recorded.

Signed-off-by: Tao Gong <gongtao0607@gmail.com>
---
--- a/mt7996/main.c
+++ b/mt7996/main.c
@@ -1713,10 +1713,17 @@ mt7996_ampdu_action(struct ieee80211_hw *hw, struct ieee80211_vif *vif,
 				   ssn, params->buf_size);
 		ret = mt7996_mcu_add_rx_ba(dev, params, vif, true);
 		break;
-	case IEEE80211_AMPDU_RX_STOP:
+	case IEEE80211_AMPDU_RX_STOP: {
+		u16 session_id = msta->deflink.rro_id[tid];
+
+		msta->deflink.rro_id[tid] = 0;
 		mt76_rx_aggr_stop(&dev->mt76, &msta->deflink.wcid, tid);
 		ret = mt7996_mcu_add_rx_ba(dev, params, vif, false);
+
+		if (session_id && mt7996_has_hwrro(dev))
+			mt7996_mcu_wed_rro_reset_sessions(dev, session_id);
 		break;
+	}
 	case IEEE80211_AMPDU_TX_OPERATIONAL:
 		mtxq->aggr = true;
 		mtxq->send_bar = false;
--- a/mt7996/mcu.c
+++ b/mt7996/mcu.c
@@ -1043,6 +1043,7 @@ mt7996_mcu_wed_rro_event(struct mt7996_dev *dev, struct sk_buff *skb)
 		while (skb->len >= sizeof(*e)) {
 			struct mt76_rx_tid *tid;
 			struct mt76_wcid *wcid;
+			struct mt7996_sta_link *msta_link;
 			u16 idx;

 			e = (void *)skb->data;
@@ -1055,10 +1056,18 @@ mt7996_mcu_wed_rro_event(struct mt7996_dev *dev, struct sk_buff *skb)
 				break;

 			tid = rcu_dereference(wcid->aggr[e->tid]);
-			if (!tid)
-				break;
+			if (!tid) {
+				/* BA session is not tracked on the primary
+				 * link (secondary link or already torn down);
+				 * keep processing the remaining events. */
+				skb_pull(skb, sizeof(*e));
+				continue;
+			}

 			tid->id = le16_to_cpu(e->id);
+			msta_link = container_of(wcid, struct mt7996_sta_link,
+						 wcid);
+			msta_link->rro_id[e->tid] = tid->id;
 			skb_pull(skb, sizeof(*e));
 		}
 		break;
--- a/mt7996/mt7996.h
+++ b/mt7996/mt7996.h
@@ -300,6 +300,12 @@ struct mt7996_sta_link {
 	unsigned long changed;

 	struct mt76_connac_sta_key_conf bip;
+	/* firmware-assigned HW_RRO session id per RX TID, reported by the
+	 * UNI_WED_RRO_BA_SESSION_STATUS event; consumed at BA teardown to
+	 * release the firmware-side session.  Written from the WED RRO event
+	 * handler, read/cleared under dev->mt76.mutex in the BA teardown
+	 * path. */
+	u16 rro_id[IEEE80211_NUM_TIDS];

 	struct {
 		u8 flowid_mask;
